        <h1
          style="font-family: 'Playfair Display', Georgia, serif; font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 2.25rem);
                 font-weight: 700; color: #0F2244; line-height: 1.25; margin: 0 0 1.5rem;"
        >
          Federal Compensation vs. Social Security Wage Maximum
        </h1>

        <div class="article-body" style="font-size: 1rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.75;">
          <!--
            Article section: Social Security wage base intro + AWI explainer + table.
            Intended placement: opening of the article, before the interactive tool.
            Figures verified against SSA (ssa.gov/OACT/COLA/cbb.html) and the SSA
            Federal Register notice for 2026 (AWI for 2024 = $69,846.57).
          -->
          <p>
            Federal compensation is a hot topic, with endless arguments over whether it is fair, whether it
            rewards performance, and how it stacks up against the private sector. I wanted to offer a
            perspective and an interactive tool comparing federal salaries against a common nationwide
            benchmark: the Social Security wage base.
          </p>
          <p>
            Let's briefly cover what the wage base is. For practical purposes it represents the maximum
            amount of wages subject to Social Security tax in a given year. Wages below the threshold are
            taxed at 6.2% paid by the employee and matched by another 6.2% paid by the employer — 12.4% in
            total, and the self-employed pay the entire 12.4% themselves. Wages above the threshold are not
            taxed for Social Security at all. Not to be confused with Medicare: the Medicare portion of your
            payroll tax has no cap whatsoever. It runs at 1.45% employee and 1.45% employer on every dollar
            you earn, with an additional 0.9% employee-only surtax on wages above $200,000 (single) or
            $250,000 (married filing jointly). So when high earners cross the wage base in the fall and see
            their take-home pay jump, the jump is only the Social Security piece stopping — Medicare keeps
            withholding all year. The wage base is also the ceiling on earnings that count toward your
            eventual benefit, which is why SSA formally calls it the contribution and benefit base. And it is
            indexed annually. As a rule of thumb, if you are earning wages above the annual threshold, you
            are doing pretty well — only about 6% of covered workers are.
          </p>
          <p>
            That annual indexing is worth understanding, because it is what makes the wage base a useful
            yardstick rather than an arbitrary number. The base is not tied to inflation — it is tied to the
            National Average Wage Index, or AWI, which is SSA's measure of what American workers actually
            earn. SSA computes it from aggregate net compensation reported on W-2s and tax returns divided by
            the number of workers, then applies that year-over-year percentage change to the prior year's AWI
            value rather than using the raw per-capita figure directly. The AWI for 2024 was $69,846.57, and
            that is the figure that set the 2026 wage base, because it takes roughly two years for the IRS
            and SSA to collect and finalize wage data from every American's tax return, so the indexing
            always runs on a two-year lag. There is one more wrinkle: the base only rises in years when a
            cost-of-living adjustment is payable. No COLA means no increase, which is why the base sat frozen
            at $106,800 from 2009 through 2011 and at $118,500 for 2015 and 2016 — and why it then jumped
            sharply once indexing resumed. The practical upshot is that the wage base tracks national wage
            growth, with a lag and an occasional pause. That makes it a reasonable, politically neutral proxy
            for what the country as a whole is earning, which is exactly what we need to compare federal
            salaries against.
          </p>

